  "slug": "alzheimer-disease-46d0",
  "summary": "Cell-type-resolved transcriptomic and multi-omic resources now enable cross-study comparison of Alzheimer's disease mechanisms in mice and humans.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is cell-type-resolved omics applied to Alzheimer's disease (AD) models and human brain. A 5xFAD snRNA-seq atlas finds VIP interneurons most disrupted in synapse programs and preferentially rescued by rolipram, implicating a Pde7b/CREB1 cAMP axis. A harmonized resource of 1,863 uniformly processed mouse cortex and hippocampus transcriptomes, including AD models, enables cross-study comparison. Lysosomal biogenesis (MiT/TFE) is reported to precede and couple to disease-associated microglia reprogramming across murine injury, amyloidosis and human AD. A meta-analysis of 1,738 human cortices links cortical miRNAs, notably miR-132-3p, to tau pathology. Developmental regulatory maps from 231 prefrontal cortices and the VEGABrain visualization tool add developmental context. The preeclampsia miRNA-mRNA study is peripheral to this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
